<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN" "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
  <overview>
    <p>Running pull request-controlled scripts or build code on private agents
    can expose persistent workspaces, credentials, and network access to contributors.
    This query requires an explicitly enabled YAML PR trigger or a simple
    PR/fork-only condition, an executable step,
    and an explicit private pool name or literal private-agent demand.</p>
    <p>Pool selection respects pipeline, stage, job, and deployment-hook overrides,
    including scalar pool names. Hosted image declarations, the Azure Pipelines
    hosted pool, server jobs, and unknown or computed pools are not classified as
    private. Omitted PR triggers and organization-level branch policies are not inferred.</p>
    <p>Literal false and selected simple Build.Reason conditions that exclude PR
    execution are recognized. Equality checks for a PullRequest build reason or
    a true System.PullRequest.IsFork value also establish explicit PR evidence.
    General condition evaluation, runtime approvals,
    external template expansion, container trust, and fork-policy settings are not
    modeled. A result indicates a PR execution path, not proof that forks are
    enabled or that every contributor is malicious. Only in-scope YAML is analyzed.</p>
  </overview>
  <recommendation>
    <p>Use isolated disposable agents for PR validation. Keep private agents with
    persistent credentials or privileged network access out of contributor-controlled
    execution paths. If private agents are required, isolate their state, minimize
    permissions, and enforce a reviewed trust boundary before privileged work.
    Checkout or workspace cleanup alone does not establish that boundary.</p>
  </recommendation>
  <references>
    <li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/yaml-schema/pool">Pool definition and precedence</a>.</li>
    <li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/security/misc">Other security considerations</a>.</li>
    <li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/829.html">CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere</a>.</li>
  </references>
</qhelp>
